## 23. PARLIAMENTARY COMMITTEES

**[SYL: GS2-C.2] | [LAXMIKANTH: Chapter 23]**

### 23.1. Introduction

Parliamentary Committees are **extra-constitutional** (mostly) devices to ensure effective parliamentary control over executive, detailed scrutiny of legislation, and expert examination of issues. They are committees of Parliamentarians.

### 23.2. CLASSIFICATION

**A. Standing Committees** - Permanent, constituted every year/elected periodically
**B. Ad hoc Committees** - Temporary, constituted for specific purpose, cease after task

### 23.3. IMPORTANT COMMITTEES

#### A. Financial Committees (most powerful)
| Committee | Composition | Function |
|---|---|---|
| **Public Accounts Committee (PAC)** | 22 members (15 LS + 7 RS), elected by proportional representation | Examines CAG reports on appropriation/accounts of Union Govt - post-expenditure control |
| **Estimates Committee** | 30 members (all from LS) | Examines estimates in budget, suggests economies/improvements - suggests alternative policies (largest committee) |
| **Committee on Public Undertakings (COPU)** | 22 members (15 LS + 7 RS) | Examines reports/accounts of Public Sector Undertakings |

#### B. Departmentally Related Standing Committees (DRSCs)
17 committees, each examines demands for grants of related ministries, bills, annual reports. **Ministers not members** - ensures scrutiny of executive.

#### C. Other Important Committees
| Committee | Role |
|---|---|
| **Committee on Privileges** | Examines breaches of privilege |
| **Committee on Petitions** | Examines petitions from public |
| **Business Advisory Committee** | Allocates time for business |
| **Rules Committee** | Frames rules of procedure |
| **Ethics Committee** | Deals with ethical issues/misconduct of members |
| **Select/Joint Committees on Bills** | Examine specific bills in detail |
| **Committee on Government Assurances** | Ensures govt fulfills assurances given to Parliament |

### 23.4. SIGNIFICANCE

- Provide **expert scrutiny** (members from different parties, can take evidence)
- Ensure **executive accountability**
- Save time of House by detailed examination
- Influence policy even though recommendations not binding (mostly accepted)
- Promote consensus

### 23.5. PYQs

| Year | Exam | Question | Topic | Approach |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 2016 | Prelims | Estimates Committee consists of members from? | Estimates Committee | Only Lok Sabha (30 members) |
| 2017 | Mains | Role of Parliamentary Committees in ensuring accountability | Committees | Financial oversight, detailed scrutiny, executive accountability |
| 2018 | Prelims | Public Accounts Committee examines reports of? | PAC | Comptroller and Auditor General of India |

### 23.6. MIND MAP

```text
PARLIAMENTARY COMMITTEES
├── FINANCIAL: PAC (CAG reports), Estimates Committee (all LS), COPU (PSUs)
├── STANDING: DRSCs (17), Privileges, Petitions, Ethics etc.
├── AD HOC: Select/Joint Committees on Bills, Inquiry Committees
└── Significance: Expert scrutiny, accountability, save House time
```

