## 26. SUPREME COURT

**[SYL: GS2-C.3] | [LAXMIKANTH: Chapter 26] | [BARE ACT: Arts. 124-147]**

### 26.1. Introduction

Supreme Court is the **apex court** of Indian judicial system. Established under Part V, Chapter IV. It is a **court of record** and has power to punish for contempt. Integrated judicial system with SC at top.

### 26.2. COMPOSITION (ART. 124)

Consists of **Chief Justice of India (CJI)** and other judges. Originally 7 judges, now sanctioned strength is **34 judges** (CJI + 33) including CJI (as per 2019 increase).

### 26.3. APPOINTMENT OF JUDGES

**Collegium System:** Judges appointed by President on **recommendation of Collegium**. Collegium headed by CJI, comprises 4 senior-most judges of SC (for appointments of SC judges). For HC judges, CJI consults collegium of HC and SC.

**National Judicial Appointments Commission (NJAC):** 99th Amendment Act, 2014 and NJAC Act, 2014 tried to replace collegium with NJAC. In ***Supreme Court Advocates-on-Record Association v. Union of India* (2015)** (NJAC judgment), SC struck down both as unconstitutional, restoring **collegium system**.

**Qualifications (Art. 124(3)):** Citizen of India, and (a) has been judge of HC for 5 years consecutively OR (b) has been advocate of HC for 10 years consecutively OR (c) distinguished jurist in opinion of President.

### 26.4. TENURE, REMOVAL, INDEPENDENCE

**Tenure:** Till age **65 years** (for SC judges). Can resign by writing to President.

**Removal (Art. 124(4)):** Only by **impeachment** for proved misbehaviour or incapacity. Process similar to President but initiated only by Parliament:
- Motion supported by **100 members of Lok Sabha** or **50 members of Rajya Sabha**
- Passed by **special majority** of both Houses
- Presented to President → order of removal

**Independence of Judiciary:** Security of tenure, fixed salaries charged on Consolidated Fund (not subject to vote), removal only by impeachment, appointments by collegium, prohibition on practice after retirement, power to punish for contempt.

### 26.5. JURISDICTION AND POWERS

#### 26.5.1. Original Jurisdiction (Art. 131)
Exclusive jurisdiction in disputes between:
- Government of India and one or more States
- Government of India and any State(s) on one side and one or more States on other
- Two or more States
**Does not extend** to disputes arising out of treaty/agreement pre-dating Constitution.

#### 26.5.2. Appellate Jurisdiction (Art. 132-134)
Hears appeals from HC in constitutional, civil and criminal cases if HC certifies fit for appeal, or under special leave to appeal (Art. 136).

#### 26.5.3. Advisory Jurisdiction (Art. 143)
President can seek opinion of SC on question of law/fact of public importance. SC **may** tender opinion (not binding).

#### 26.5.4. Special Leave to Appeal (Art. 136)
**Discretionary jurisdiction** - SC can grant special leave to appeal from any judgment/order of any court/tribunal (except military tribunals). Widest appellate power.

#### 26.5.5. Writ Jurisdiction (Art. 32)
SC can issue writs (**Habeas Corpus, Mandamus, Prohibition, Certiorari, Quo Warranto**) for **enforcement of Fundamental Rights**. Art. 32 is itself a Fundamental Right - called "**heart and soul of Constitution**" by Dr. Ambedkar.

#### 26.5.6. Other Powers
- Court of Record (Art. 129) - can punish for contempt
- Judicial review
- Power to do complete justice (Art. 142) - can pass any order/decree to do complete justice in any cause/pending matter
- Guardian of Constitution
- Public Interest Litigation (PIL)

### 26.6. JUDICIAL REVIEW

Power to examine constitutionality of legislative/executive actions and declare them void if unconstitutional. Basis in Constitution (Arts. 13, 32, 141, 142, 226). Developed through judicial interpretation. Essential for federalism and protection of FRs. Subject to **Basic Structure** doctrine (*Kesavananda Bharati*).

### 26.7. PYQs

| Year | Exam | Question | Topic | Approach |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 2016 | Prelims | Art. 32 is called heart and soul of Constitution by? | Writ Jurisdiction | Dr. B.R. Ambedkar |
| 2017 | Mains | Judicial activism vs judicial restraint | Judicial Activism | Balance needed, PIL expansion |
| 2018 | Prelims | NJAC judgment is in which case? | Appointments | SCARA v. Union of India (2015) struck down NJAC |
| 2019 | Prelims | Special Leave to Appeal is under which Article? | Art. 136 | Article 136 - discretionary power |
| 2020 | Mains | Article 142 - complete justice | Powers | Wide powers to do complete justice |

### 26.8. MIND MAP

```text
SUPREME COURT (ARTS. 124-147)
├── COMPOSITION
│   ├── CJI + 33 other judges (total 34)
│   └── Appointed by Collegium (NJAC struck down in 2015)
├── JURISDICTION
│   ├── Original (Art. 131) - Centre-State disputes
│   ├── Appellate (Arts. 132-134)
│   ├── Advisory (Art. 143) - opinion to President
│   ├── Special Leave (Art. 136) - discretionary
│   └── Writ (Art. 32) - enforcement of FRs
├── POWERS
│   ├── Judicial Review
│   ├── Art. 142 - complete justice
│   ├── Court of Record, contempt power
│   └── Guardian of Constitution
└── INDEPENDENCE
    ├── Security of tenure, impeachment only
    └── Salaries charged on Consolidated Fund
```

